---
description: graphify knowledge graph — when to query it instead of grepping
alwaysApply: true
---

This repository has a graphify code graph at `graphify-out/graph.json`, built by
`bash scripts/graphify_bootstrap.sh` (AST-only, no LLM, ~7 s). It is a derived
artifact and is git-ignored, so it may be absent — in that case just work
normally, or build it if the question below is the kind the graph answers well.

**Use the graph first for questions about structure**, where grep gives you
matches but not relationships:

- `graphify query "<question>"` — scoped subgraph for an architecture or
  navigation question
- `graphify path "<A>" "<B>"` — dependency path between two symbols
- `graphify affected "<X>"` — reverse traversal: what breaks if `X` changes
- `graphify explain "<concept>"` — nodes related to one concept
- `graphify god-nodes` — the most connected symbols, i.e. where the coupling is

The graph carries inferred cross-file edges — `calls`, `inherits`, `mixes_in` —
that no single grep will surface, and `# WHY:` comments and ADR citations are
first-class nodes linked to the code. That is the reason to reach for it before
reconstructing the same picture by hand.

**Read, Grep and Glob stay the right tool** for reading a specific file you have
already located, for exact-string searches, and for anything outside the graphed
corpus. Do not route a one-file question through the graph.

**Corpus boundary:** `.graphifyignore` excludes `docs/notes/`, `docs/prompts/`,
`docs/archive/` and `tests/` — session notes and prompts are history, not
structure, and including them tripled build time for no navigational gain.
`docs/adr/`, `docs/contracts/`, `docs/runbooks/`, `docs/guides/` and
`docs/quality/` **are** in the graph, so architectural decisions are reachable
from the code that implements them.

After changing code, `graphify update .` keeps the graph current — incremental,
no API cost. A stale graph is worse than no graph: it answers confidently about
a structure that no longer exists.
